<h1>The history of systems that tried to explain everything</h1>
<p class="lede">The intellectual spine: every previous total explanatory system — who built it, when, and what became of it — and then what the last hundred years has tried instead. This is a curated overview; the full, sourced account is in <a href="{{ '/docs/history_of_systems_theories/' | url }}">HISTORY</a>, and the living contemporary field in <a href="{{ '/docs/landscape_of_contemporary_systems_theories/' | url }}">LANDSCAPE</a>.</p>

<p>By "systems theory" here we mean something broader than the twentieth-century academic field of that name: any <strong>total explanatory framework</strong> — a way of accounting, in a single coherent order, for nature, society, mind, history, and meaning together. For most of recorded history, human beings <em>had</em> such systems. That sense of coherent, total understanding — the thing that today feels lost — was, for millennia, the normal condition.</p>

<h2>The age of total systems (antiquity to ~1830)</h2>
<p>Greek philosophy of nature invented the founding move: the search for the <em>archē</em>, a single underlying principle — Thales's water, Heraclitus's <em>logos</em> and flux, Parmenides's unchanging Being, the atomists' void and atoms. Then the two poles: <strong>Plato</strong> (c. 428–348 BCE) gives totality <em>from above</em> — the world of Forms, the Good as the sun of the intelligible, the soul and city ordered alike; the Cave is still the sharpest image of a mediated, manufactured reality. <strong>Aristotle</strong> (384–322 BCE) gives it <em>from below</em> — the first encyclopedic attempt to categorise all knowledge (logic, physics, metaphysics, biology, ethics, politics, poetics), bound by the four causes and teleology. He is the model of the system built bottom-up from observation, and the founder of the idea that the whole can be <em>mapped</em>.</p>
<p>The Stoics held the cosmos as one living system pervaded by <em>logos</em>; Plotinus built the great emanationist hierarchy from the One. In parallel — often deeper — ran the non-Western totalities: Chinese correlative cosmology (yin–yang, the five phases, the Dao; Zhu Xi's <em>li</em> and <em>qi</em>), the Buddhist <em>pratītyasamutpāda</em> (dependent origination) and Huayan's Indra's Net of mutually-reflecting jewels, Advaita Vedānta's one Brahman, and the vast Aristotelian-Neoplatonic systems of Avicenna, Averroes, and Maimonides.</p>
<p>In the Latin West the total system reached its most complete form in <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (1225–1274) and the <em>Summa</em>: one architecture integrating God, cosmos, human nature, ethics, law, and salvation — the "Great Chain of Being," a hierarchical, meaningful, complete world in which everything had its place. This is the high-water mark of <em>lived</em> total understanding.</p>
<p>The Scientific Revolution did not abandon the ambition; it <em>transferred</em> it from theology to mathematics and mechanism — <strong>Descartes</strong> (a unified system deduced from first principles), <strong>Spinoza</strong> (the <em>Ethics</em>, built <em>more geometrico</em> on a single substance, God-or-Nature), <strong>Leibniz</strong> (the Monadology — and, prophetically, the dream of a <em>calculus ratiocinator</em> that would let all truths be <em>computed</em>, the distant ancestor of holding a system computationally rather than in one head), and <strong>Newton</strong> (the <em>Principia</em>: the mathematical system of the world). The Enlightenment's <em>Encyclopédie</em> tried to systematise all human knowledge.</p>

<h2>The apex and the inversion (~1780–1900)</h2>
<p>German Idealism was the last great flowering. <strong>Kant</strong> (1724–1804) built the critical system — the three <em>Critiques</em> mapping the conditions of knowledge, morality, and judgement — and made the decisive, double-edged move: the order we find in the world is partly imposed <em>by the structure of the knowing subject</em>. He also drew the boundary (reason cannot know things-in-themselves), planting the seed of every later doubt about totality. Then <strong>Hegel</strong> (1770–1831), the apex: the dialectical system that aims to comprehend the entire of reality — logic, nature, history, art, religion, philosophy — as the self-unfolding of the Absolute Idea. <strong>By wide agreement Hegel is the last thinker to claim a complete rational system of the whole in the grand classical sense.</strong> After him the system fractures; everything that follows completes, inverts, or refuses him.</p>
<p>The nineteenth century then produced three or four <em>post-theological</em> total systems — <strong>Marx</strong> (historical materialism: history driven by modes of production and class struggle), <strong>Darwin</strong> and <strong>Spencer</strong> (evolution stretched, disastrously, into a "synthetic philosophy" of everything), and <strong>Comte</strong> (positivism, the law of three stages, and an explicit Religion of Humanity to do theology's integrating work). By 1900 there were rival totalities in play, and the sense that <em>one</em> of them might be the key to everything.</p>

<h2>The crisis: why the total system broke (~1880–1970)</h2>
<p>Then, within a few decades, five hammers fell:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Nietzsche</strong> (1880s) — "God is dead," and with him any guaranteed total meaning; and an attack on the systematic impulse itself: <em>"the will to a system is a lack of integrity."</em> He named the disease we still have no cure for — nihilism, the situation in which the highest values devalue themselves.</li>
  <li><strong>Gödel's incompleteness</strong> (1931) — any formal system rich enough for arithmetic must be incomplete or inconsistent. Not a metaphor but a theorem: no sufficiently rich system can fully capture itself.</li>
  <li><strong>Specialisation</strong> — the "last person who knew everything" became impossible; knowledge fissioned into hundreds of languages, and the cognitive precondition of the old systems evaporated.</li>
  <li><strong>Heidegger</strong> (1927 onward) — a total diagnosis of the modern epoch: technology as <em>Gestell</em>, "enframing," under which everything, humans included, shows up as optimisable standing-reserve. He named the deep logic our own theories try to make precise.</li>
  <li><strong>The refusal of grand narratives</strong> — <strong>Lyotard</strong> (1979): <em>"incredulity toward metanarratives."</em> Foucault replaced total theory with genealogies of power/knowledge; Derrida undid every text's closure; Baudrillard described simulation and hyperreality where the map precedes the territory.</li>
</ul>
<p>So <em>since when have we lacked total systems?</em> In the grand classical sense, since roughly <strong>Hegel (d. 1831)</strong>, with Whitehead's <em>Process and Reality</em> (1929) usually named the last serious attempt in the old mode; as a <em>shared, lived</em> understanding, it eroded across the twentieth century and was declared over by the postmodern turn.</p>

<h2>The last hundred years: the ambition did not die — it migrated</h2>
<p>Here is the correction the project insists on. It is <em>true</em> that the classical grand system died; it is <em>false</em> that "no one has tried since Heidegger." The ambition changed form and migrated out of philosophy.</p>
<p><strong>Into a new science of systems as such.</strong> Bogdanov's <em>Tektology</em> (1912–17); <strong>Bertalanffy's</strong> General System Theory (open systems, emergence, isomorphies across domains); <strong>Wiener's</strong> cybernetics (feedback, control, communication) and Shannon's information theory; second-order cybernetics and Maturana &amp; Varela's <em>autopoiesis</em>; systems dynamics from Forrester to <strong>Donella Meadows</strong> (<em>Limits to Growth</em>, and the still-indispensable toolkit of stocks, flows, feedback, delay, and leverage points); Prigogine's dissipative structures (order out of chaos, far from equilibrium); complexity science (Santa Fe: emergence, networks, scaling laws); and <strong>Edgar Morin's</strong> <em>pensée complexe</em> — who also coined <em>polycrisis</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Into social systems theory.</strong> The most direct rebuttal to "no one tried" is a sociologist: <strong>Niklas Luhmann</strong> (1927–1998), who took autopoiesis and built an <em>explicitly universal</em> theory of society — not made of people but of communication, differentiated into self-referential subsystems (law, economy, science, art, politics, media), each closed and unable to steer the others. It speaks with eerie directness to coordination failure and the fragmentation of shared reality.</p>
<p><strong>Into new syntheses and grand narratives, most carrying warning labels.</strong> <strong>Big History</strong> (David Christian — a single narrative from the Big Bang, organised by rising complexity); <strong>Integral Theory</strong> (Ken Wilber — an explicit "theory of everything," influential and contested); <strong>metamodernism</strong> (Vermeulen &amp; van den Akker; Hanzi Freinacht; Jason Josephson Storm — totality held <em>provisionally</em>, "reconstruction after deconstruction," a never-final proto-synthesis — close to this project's own stance); <strong>cliodynamics</strong> (<strong>Peter Turchin</strong> — history as a natural science; the structural-demographic model of elite overproduction, popular immiseration, and the "wealth pump," with a falsifiable 2010 forecast of instability peaking in the 2020s); and the <strong>polycrisis</strong> discourse (Morin's term, revived by Tooze and the WEF) — which, even its champions concede, <em>names</em> the entanglement without <em>explaining</em> it. <strong>That gap — a name for the entanglement but no rigorous systems theory of it — is exactly the niche this project targets.</strong> Alongside run the sharp single-mechanism diagnoses of the present as system: Zuboff (surveillance capitalism), Han (the burnout/achievement subject), Lembke (the engineered-reward economy), Latour and Morton (dissolving the nature/society divide; hyperobjects too vast to perceive).</p>

<h2>Where that leaves the founding premise</h2>
<p>Partly right, and more interesting once corrected. <strong>Right:</strong> the classical mode — one mind, one book, one complete rational system — is genuinely dead. <strong>Too strong:</strong> the <em>ambition</em> did not die; it migrated into systems science, Luhmann's social theory, and the contemporary syntheses. Why it is genuinely hard now: Gödelian limits, knowledge overload, pace and reflexivity (theory changes the system it describes), the moral suspicion of totality after the totalitarian century, genuine computational irreducibility, and the meaning vacuum (systems thinking is good at <em>how</em> and bad at <em>why-it-matters</em>). Why a new method might nonetheless work: computation and modelling, network and complexity science at scale, commons-based authorship, and <strong>designed disagreement</strong> — a procedural rather than doctrinal totality, never declared finished. The rest of the project acts on that wager and keeps an honest record of whether it works.</p>

